Redwood JS an opinionated full stack  JavaScript framework for building big  ambitious startups as we all know the  JavaScript ecosystem is incredibly  complex with millions of different tools  to choose from when architecting an app  Redwood takes a collection of the most  battle tested Technologies like react  Prisma graphql storybook and jest then  weaves them together so developers can  get things done quickly and reliably it  was developed by a team of software  Engineers that includes the co-founder  of GitHub and hit version 1.0 in 2022 it  has its own custom router for  server-side rendering but unlike many  other Frameworks it doesn't use the file  system and instead puts all routes in a  single file with automatic code  splitting for each page most importantly  though it provides abstractions to  connect your front-end UI to a back-end  relational database via a graphql API  its rails inspired CLI can quickly  generate the code required to build  complex features and not just build them  but also test them with jest and  storybook it ships with Apollo client by  default making it easy for the front end  to consume data from the graphql API and  when it comes time for deployment the  code can easily run on a standalone  server or your favorite serverless  infrastructure to get started generate a  new Redwood project with yarn the front  end code is managed in the web directory  it's a react app that's wrapped with the  Redwood provider with routes as the main  entry point for the UI the routes  component is a collection of URL  patterns that point to a specific  component which is defined in the Pages  directory all pages are automatically  imported here keeping your code nice and  concise in addition all pages are  automatically lazy loaded with code  splitting making your load times as fast  as possible it also contains a layout  system to share UI across different  routes now the back end is organized in  the API directory which uses Prisma to  manage the database schema but also  provides the tools required to build a  full-fledged graphql API to access your  data the back end can also Define its  own custom serverless functions or  directives which operate like middleware  to handle concerns like user  authentication what's really cool though  is Redwood provides an abstraction  called a cell to simplify complex data  fetching a cell is just a component that  manages a graphql query allowing you to  clearly Define different states like  loading error and success like angular  or rails Redwood provides strong  conventions and a CLI to generate the  boilerplate code for you use it to  quickly create a component page or cell  along with the code for jest and  storybook for testing and finally use  the CLI to deploy the app to your  favorite serverless Cloud platform this  has been Redwood JS in 100 seconds hit  the like button and subscribe if you  want to see more short videos like this  thanks for watching and I will see you  in the next one
 

